== Subinterpreters vs Multiprocessing
pygrametl already provides support for parallel ETL processing through multiprocessing, allowing different parts of an ETL pipeline to execute concurrently @pygrametl-docs. With the introduction of the `concurrent.interpreters` module in Python 3.14 (PEP 734), subinterpreters have become a more accessible alternative for achieving parallel execution. Unlike multiprocessing, subinterpreters operate within the same process while maintaining separate interpreter states and Global Interpreter Locks (GILs), allowing CPU-bound Python code to execute in parallel across multiple cores @pep734. This motivated an investigation into whether subinterpreters could provide performance advantages over multiprocessing for workloads relevant to pygrametl.

To compare the two approaches, we developed seven standalone microbenchmarks focusing on different aspects of parallel execution. We evaluated communication overhead by measuring the transfer throughput of different Python objects, including dictionaries of varying sizes to represent ETL rows. We also examined task distribution across multiple workers and coordination through a shared component responsible for generating unique identifiers. Additionally, we measured the scalability of CPU-bound workloads, the performance and memory consumption of sharing a large read-only buffer, and the overhead associated with worker creation. The experiments were conducted using Python 3.14.7 on a machine with eight logical CPUs. Worker counts ranged from one to eight, and each experiment was repeated multiple times, with median execution times reported. Where applicable, workers were reused to isolate execution performance from initialisation overhead.

The results showed that both approaches achieved similar CPU-bound performance, with multiprocessing reaching a speed-up of 4.53 and subinterpreters 4.63 when using eight workers. Subinterpreters also performed better when distributing independent tasks, completing the fan-out benchmark approximately 27% faster with eight workers. Additionally, subinterpreters generally demonstrated lower memory consumption, using 89 MB compared with 129 MB for multiprocessing in the eight-worker CPU benchmark.

However, multiprocessing performed considerably better when transferring dictionaries, which is particularly relevant as pygrametl represents ETL rows using this data structure. For dictionaries containing ten columns, multiprocessing queues achieved approximately 210,000 rows per second, compared with 105,000 for subinterpreter queues. As shown in @fig:dict-transfer, this performance difference was consistent across all tested dictionary sizes. Multiprocessing also performed better in the shared identifier coordination benchmark, where the default subinterpreter implementation was substantially slower due to a queue polling delay. Removing this delay significantly improved subinterpreter performance, although multiprocessing remained faster at eight workers. Finally, creating eight workers took approximately 4.23 ms using process forking, compared with 56.80 ms for subinterpreters.

Based on these results, we decided not to pursue a subinterpreter-based implementation for pygrametl. Given that pygrametl already provides an established multiprocessing implementation, our experiments did not demonstrate sufficient benefits to justify replacing it with subinterpreters. We therefore chose to focus our further investigation on other approaches to improving parallel ETL performance.
